                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Here's an honest read on Priyanshu's resume:\n\n**Strengths**\n- Clear tech stack listings for each project — recruiters like seeing concrete tools.\n- Decent skill breadth (DSA, ML basics, web frameworks, cloud) for someone still in college.\n- Academic percentages are strong (93%, 85%).\n\n**What's holding it back**\n\n1. **No internship or work experience section.** If he's had any internship, freelance work, or even a significant open-source contribution, it needs its own section — that carries more weight than projects alone.\n\n2. **Projects lack impact/metrics.** Right now it's all \"developed,\" \"implemented,\" \"designed\" — no numbers. Even rough ones help: \"reduced categorization time by X%,\" \"processed data for X users,\" \"handled X API requests.\" Recruiters skim for outcomes, not just descriptions.\n\n3. **Missing standard sections**: no LinkedIn/GitHub/portfolio link, no \"Objective/Summary\" (optional but common), no certifications section if he has any (AWS, Coursera, etc. — he lists AWS as a tool but no certification backs it up).\n\n4. **Formatting issues from the PDF extraction**: ligature glitches (\"ApplicaƟon\" instead of \"Application\") suggest a font/encoding problem in the actual PDF — this needs fixing before sending anywhere, since it'll look broken in some PDF viewers or ATS systems.\n\n5. **\"Coursework/Skills\" is a mixed bag.** Combine into a cleaner \"Technical Skills\" section split into Languages / Frameworks / Tools / Databases (he's close, just clean the label).\n\n6. **Hobbies section is weak filler.** \"Solving coding problems\" and \"exploring ML\" essentially restate his skills section — either cut this section or replace with something that shows personality (real hobbies) if space allows.\n\n7. **No dates on projects.** Add month/year for each project so recruiters can gauge recency and progression.\n\n**Overall**: Solid foundation for a fresher/intern-level resume — the projects are relevant and the stack is current — but it reads a bit generic right now. Tightening the bullet points with quantifiable impact, fixing the encoding glitch, and adding a GitHub/LinkedIn link would meaningfully improve it.\n\nWant me to help rewrite the bullet points with stronger, metric-driven phrasing?"
